        "descriptions": [
          {
            "lang": "en-Us",
            "description": "This project contains qualitative data from 20 semi-structured interviews conducted with community members, local leaders, and individuals involved in disaster response and recovery efforts in Western North Carolina following Hurricane Helene. Hurricane Helene impacted the region in September 2024, producing widespread inland flooding, infrastructure damage, and communication disruptions associated with both the storm and its predecessor rain event (PRE). Interviews were conducted in November 2025 to examine how residents experienced, interpreted, and responded to the disaster over time. Participants were asked about their experiences before, during, and after Hurricane Helene, with particular attention to risk perception, warning interpretation, decision-making, and access to information and resources. Interviews explored how individuals understood hurricane and flood risk, how they evaluated official weather information and warnings, and how social relationships influenced protective actions and recovery processes. Participants also reflected on communication challenges, infrastructure failures, mutual aid efforts, institutional trust, and the role of family members, neighbors, community organizations, and government agencies during the disaster. This dataset may support research in disaster preparedness, emergency management, hazard mitigation, qualitative disaster studies, and weather risk communication. It may also be useful for policymakers, emergency managers, meteorologists, and community organizations seeking to improve warning communication, community resilience, and disaster response strategies for inland and hurricane-affected communities.",
            "descriptionType": "Abstract"
          }
